/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The TCU needs two numbers before it can choose a gear: throttle opening α (percent, 0 means closed, 100 means wide open) and vehicle speed v (km/h, from the output shaft sensor or the wheel speed sensors).
   - Plot α on the vertical axis and v on the horizontal axis.
   - Light throttle keeps the curves at low speed, so the car upshifts early and the engine stays slow and economical.
   - Every decision rests on one relation:
   - nengine = nwheel × igear × ifinal
   - nengine is engine speed in rpm; nwheel is wheel speed in rpm; igear is the selected gear ratio; ifinal is the final drive ratio.
   - An overdrive gear has igear below 1, so it lowers engine speed.
   - A shift is hydraulic work.
   - The same loop drives four different mechanisms.
   - The torque converter is an engine-driven impeller throwing oil at a turbine on the gearbox input, so the car can creep at idle with no mechanical link.
*/
