/* book exam traps: Leaving out the final drive when converting wheel speed to engine speed. Engine rpm needs both ratios: gear ratio and final drive. | Assuming a shift happens at a fixed vehicle speed. The map also reads throttle, so a light throttle upshifts early and a heavy one holds the gear. | Getting the ratio direction wrong. igear above 1 reduces speed and multiplies torque; an overdrive below 1 does the opposite. | Confusing the lock-up clutch inside the torque converter with the shift clutches. Lock-up only removes converter slip; the shift clutches change the ratio. | Mixing rpm and rad/s, or forgetting the ×60 and ÷60 in the wheel-speed conversion. */
