/* book: lead: Cruise control keeps a car at a speed the driver chooses, without the driver holding the pedal. It measures actual speed, compares it with the set speed, and moves the throttle through a controller. Adaptive cruise control (ACC) adds a forward sensor: it also measures the gap to the car ahead and slows or brakes to keep a safe time gap. Used on highways, in stop-and-go traffic, and in many new car */
/* book Figure 5.2.1: The ACC gap of the worked example. The lead car is 60 m ahead, 10 m beyond the 50 m target; if nothing changes the gap closes in 12 s, while a steady 1.25 m/s² brake settles the host at 20 m/s and 50 m after 4 s. */
