/* book exam traps: More assist is not always better: assist must fall as vehicle speed rises, otherwise the steering feels numb and the driver loses road feel. | Steering torque is the driver's input measured by the torsion bar; assist torque is the motor's output at the pinion. They are not the same quantity. | Going from the motor to the pinion, multiply by N; going back from the assist the map asks for to the motor torque, divide by N, as the worked example does. Using the wrong direction makes the current 256 times wrong for a 16 : 1 gear. | The torque sensor measures twist across the torsion bar; the steering angle sensor measures absolute wheel position. Swapping their roles breaks return-to-centre and ESP. | Fail-safe does not mean the motor keeps assisting. It means the motor is disconnected and the driver still has a mechanical path to the road wheels. */
